  set:  # ba8f
    description: "FILED 2026-09-10 from WI-pokam, whose cheap half shipped in PR #892 while this,\nthe larger half, did not. Absorbs WI-hijot (INV-dihos Phase 3, Scala return-type\nregistry), folded in 2026-09-30.\n\nWHAT IT IS. `scala.py` types a receiver only from a DECLARED type -- a parameter\nannotation (`_extract_param_types_scala`), a val annotation or a `new`\n(`_declared_type_name` on `val_definition`), a class parameter. Scala's\ninference is pervasive and idiomatic code writes none of these:\n\n    val x = mk()                    <- the return type of `mk`\n    for (y <- xs) { y.foo() }       <- a for-comprehension binder\n    xs.map(z => z.foo())            <- a lambda parameter\n    a.b().c()                       <- a chained receiver\n\nNone of them carries a declared type, so none reaches `var_types` and every\nreceiver among them falls to the sentinel. Fixture check (2026-09-30): all four\nemit scala:external:0-0:<method>; the annotated `val y: FileWriter = mk()`\ncontrol is typed scala:java.io.FileWriter.\n\nWHERE IT STANDS AFTER PR #892, measured on this tree at filing (not re-measured\nsince):\n\n    repo   hinted share BEFORE   AFTER   external method edges\n    sbt          9.03%          23.43%          15,872\n    lila         4.50%           8.02%          38,008\n\nSo lila is still at 8% -- barely out of the 5-10% band WI-pokam was filed on --\nand the remaining ~92% is this item. The type-node fix took the shapes that WERE\ndeclared and merely mis-parsed; what is left needs INFERENCE.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS ALREADY BUILT, AND HALF OF IT IS INHERITED. The base\nTreeSitterAnalyzer aggregates every file's `FileAnalysis.method_return_types`\ninto `self._method_return_type_registry` and merges the language's\nlibrary_signatures rows after them (base.py, step \"4c\"); swift fills it in\nPass 1 and reads it for a call-result binding. objc keeps its own\n`method_return_type_registry` (passed to its `_extract_edges`). ScalaAnalyzer\nsubclasses TreeSitterAnalyzer but never fills `method_return_types` and never\nreads the registry, and there is no library_signatures/scala.yaml.\n\nDELIVERABLES (the call-result / chained half is WI-hijot's prescription):\n1. Pass 1: populate `FileAnalysis.method_return_types` from each\n   function_definition's declared return type.\n2. A `_extract_scala_return_type_name` helper: `def f(...): T` extraction;\n   generic strip (`List[T]` / `Map[K, V]` -> `List` / `Map`; scala writes `[T]`);\n   unwrap of `Option[T]` / `Try[T]` / `Future[T]` / `Either[L, R]` (right-biased),\n   analogous to Rust's Option/Result. Implicits explicitly deferred.\n3. Pass 2: consult `self._method_return_type_registry` when filling `var_types`\n   for a call-result val, and add a typed-variable / chained-receiver method-call\n   strategy in the edge extractor, mirroring Rust's.\n4. For-binder and lambda-parameter receivers: need an element type from the\n   collection's declared type; not covered by the registry alone.\nLibrary rows (Runtime.getRuntime().exec) are WI-jubim, separate.\n\nSIZE THE SHAPES BEFORE BUILDING. Enumerate the untyped-receiver population on\nsbt and lila by the SYNTAX that bound the receiver (call result, for-binder,\nlambda parameter, chained receiver, match binding, `implicit` parameter) before\ndesigning. A one-dimensional frame cannot predict its own counterexamples.\n\nPRECISION WARNING, EARNED IN PR #892 AND NOT HYPOTHETICAL. Widening what gets a\nhint widens what can reach the bare SHORT-NAME BIND, which is the funnel\n`scala.py` refuses for untyped receivers. That PR's first version bound 44 of 185\nnewly-resolved sbt edges to a project `Map.get` when the receiver was a\nstandard-library `Map[K,V]`. Any return-type work must count its newly-RESOLVED\nedges by owner, not just its newly-hinted ones -- the item's nominal metric (the\nhinted share) rewarded the WORSE version there and will do so again.\n\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # ba8f
